# THE RULED SPELLING OF A LITERAL BRACE. THIS FILE IS AN EXECUTABLE RATCHET.
#
# `docs/text-law.md` rules that `\{` and `\}` are the literal-brace spelling in
# the canonical text face. The `# expect:` line below states the RULED answer.
#
# The exact current compiler preserves the escaped opening brace through
# interpolation: the first line prints `v={x}`, while the second still resolves
# its unescaped `{x}` hole. The source oracle therefore describes the measured
# answer. Any native ledger row that still calls this source `fail` is stale
# evidence and must be reclassified only by the exact paired run.
#
# WHAT MUST NOT HAPPEN INSTEAD: the second line must keep interpolating. A
# spelling that makes a literal brace possible by making the hole impossible has
# not solved anything, so both readings appear in one literal here.
# expect: v={x} both 7 and {x} pair={"k": 1}
# @corpus current

x = 7

# The escaped hole is literal text.
print("v=\{x}")

# One literal, both readings — the case no raw face can serve, because a raw
# face has no holes. 128 sites across 17 files in the compiler corpus are this
# shape; `docs/text-law.md` enumerates them.
print("both {x} and \{x}")

# A brace pair that is not an expression at all. This is the control: the
# ruling must preserve its source text without disabling real holes.
print("pair=\{\"k\": 1\}")
0
